<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gandzasar Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1216">1216</a>–<a href="/years/1238">1238</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gandzasar is an Armenian monastery in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>, built for Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-jalal-dawla">Hasan-Jalal Dawla</a> of Khachen, whose Cathedral of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> was consecrated in 1240. It served for centuries as the seat of the Catholicos of Aghvank and the burial place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hasan-jalalyan">Hasan-Jalalyan</a> princes. Its carved stonework makes it one of the finest monuments of medieval Armenian architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gandzasar-monastery.jpg" alt="Gandzasar Monastery" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vahagn Grigoryan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DJI_-Gandzasar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Gandzasar means treasure mountain in Armenian and is believed to come from a tradition that the monastery stands on a hill with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and other metal ores. The site is first mentioned by the 10th-century Catholicos Anania of Moks, who listed Sargis, a monk of Gandzasar, among the participants of a council held at Khachen in 949. Khachkars dated 1174, 1182 and 1202 found around the monastery also point to an earlier church or monastery.</p><p>The main church was built between 1216 and 1238 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-jalal-dawla">Hasan-Jalal Dawla</a>, prince of Inner Khachen, and consecrated on 22 July 1240, the Feast of the Transfiguration, with some 700 priests present. The narthex was started in 1240 and finished in 1266 by his son Atabek and Atabek's mother Mamkan. The contemporary historian Kirakos Gandzaketsi described the building in his History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The monastery stands on a hill at 1,270 metres, south-west of the village of Vank. Its walled complex has the church, narthex, living quarters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s residence, refectory and school. The church, named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, has a cruciform plan with two-floored sacristies at the corners and is known for a richly decorated sixteen-sided dome. Reliefs on its exterior show the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion">Crucifixion</a>, Mary with the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, Adam and Eve, two patrons holding a model of the church, a bull's head and an eagle, and have been compared to the carvings of Aghtamar. Anatoli Yakobson called it an encyclopedia of Armenian architecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 14th century Gandzasar became the seat of the Catholicosate of Caucasian Albania, a see of the Armenian Apostolic Church, and in the 16th century it became subordinate to Etchmiadzin. In the early 1700s its patriarch is said to have had authority over some 900 villages. Under Catholicos Yesayi Hasan-Jalalyan it was a centre of efforts by the Karabakh Armenian meliks to seek Russian protection. A Russian regulation of 1836 moved the diocese of Artsakh to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>, and the monastery decayed. The Soviet authorities closed it by 1930, and it reopened on 1 October 1989 under Archbishop Pargev Martirosyan.</p><p>During the First Nagorno-Karabakh War Azerbaijani helicopters bombarded the complex on 16 August 1992, and an air strike in January 1993 damaged it seriously. It was refurbished between 2000 and 2002 with funding from Levon Hayrapetyan. After the Azerbaijani offensive of 19 and 20 September 2023, Azerbaijani authorities described it as a Caucasian Albanian monument, which Armenian officials, the Armenian Church and scholars such as Victor Schnirelmann reject, pointing to its Armenian inscriptions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gandzasar monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The meliks maintained churches and the <b>Gandzasar</b> catholicosate.</blockquote><a href="/state/melikdoms-of-karabakh">Melikdoms of Karabakh</a></li><li><blockquote>The greatest, Hasan-Jalal Dawla, prince from 1214, built the monastery of <b>Gandzasar</b>, seat of the Catholicos of Aghvank.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-khachen">Principality of Khachen</a></li></ul></section>
